    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper tests monitoring of emergent misalignment during supervised finetuning, not refusal loss, first-token concentration, bypasses, or safety localization. Its behavioral outcomes concern a related but different construct.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports Betley emergent-misalignment rates after finetuning, including a matched benign-versus-dangerous control and a LoRA-rank ablation. These are recorded under fits_no_version because they measure broad emergent misalignment, not refusal rates on harmful requests or any of the other claim versions. The monitor's detection scores, representation geometry, steering validation, and cited findings are not outcomes of the claim versions.",
    "test_reason": "The study builds and tests an activation-space monitor for emergent misalignment (EM) during SFT. Its behavioural ground truth is the Betley EM rate on open-ended benign prompts, and refusals are excluded from scoring. It never measures refusal of or compliance with harmful requests, per-token KL, prefill or decoding bypasses, safety localization, or deepening. Qi et al. is cited only in the introduction, as evidence that loss is blind to misalignment. Its behavioural results concern a related but different construct (broad EM), so they go under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct as paraphrased. The authors conclude that trait-space drift supports checkpoint-level EM monitoring (1.8% FNR, 2.0% FPR held-out), that the monitor needs calibration, and that recalibration is needed across architecture, update method or starting alignment state.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study uses LoRA SFT and full finetuning on emergent-misalignment datasets and scores broad Betley EM; it does not use the original safety fine-tuning/refusal-rate, per-token KL, or compliant-prefill procedures.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Monitor calibration and operating point can shift with extraction prompts, architecture, update method, training horizon, and starting alignment state. LoRA and full-finetuning drift kinetics differ.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "With matched prompts, dangerous extreme_sports completions gave 7.4–26.4% pooled EM versus 0–0.5% for safe completions, across four 7–9B models.",
        "proposed_claim": "Completion content, not fine-tuning per se, drives broad emergent misalignment in these models.",
